    "case_summary": "A 50-year-old Indian woman with diabetes and hypertension presented with right upper abdominal discomfort, loss of appetite, and low-grade fever. She denied jaundice.  \nOn examination, she had hepatomegaly and tenderness in the right hypochondrium.  \nLaboratory studies showed normal liver-function tests. Serological tests for amoebiasis and hydatid disease were negative. A chest radiograph was normal.  \nAbdominal ultrasonography revealed an 11 × 7.5 × 11.2 cm predominantly anechoic, thick-walled lesion arising from the inferior surface of the liver, with low-level internal echoes and a homogeneous echogenic area in its superolateral portion. The gallbladder was not visualized separately; other abdominal viscera were normal.  \nAspiration of the lesion yielded turbid fluid, but the lesion persisted despite repeated aspirations and antibiotic therapy.  \nContrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen demonstrated a well-defined, lobulated cystic lesion with a solid component in its superior part, abutting the colon and duodenum. The lesion was interpreted as an infected non-parasitic cyst.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. biliary cystadenocarcinoma\nB. because of right upper quadrant pain, fever, imaging, and turbid aspirate\nC. Liver abscess was initially suspected because of right upper quadrant pain, fever, imaging, and turbid aspirate\nD. Parasitic cysts (amebic or hydatid) were considered given epidemiology and cystic appearance, but were excluded\nE. Simple hepatic cyst was considered; however, the lesion showed a thick wall, internal echoes, and a solid component\nF. Infected non\nG. Biliary cystadenoma was diagnosed after initial cyst wall excision\nH. Biliary cystadenocarcinoma was identified on further histology due to malignant transformation and infiltration\n",
